$\begin{align}
\dfrac{1}{y}&=8-x
\\\\
-\dfrac{1}{y^2}\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dt}&=-\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$

We are given that $\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-0.5$, and we want to find $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ when $y=0.2$.

Let's plug $\blueD{y=0.2}$ and $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-0.5}$ into the equation we obtained:

$\begin{align}
-\dfrac{1}{\blueD{y}^2}\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}}&=-\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-\dfrac{1}{\blueD{0.2}^2}(\goldD{-0.5})&=-\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-12.5&=\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$
